WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:08.280
Let's move up the stack a little bit and talk about application infrastructure.

2
00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:13.280
On your phone or personal computer, you typically launch an application to interact with it,

3
00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:18.280
especially if it's something like a game or a browser or a word processing program.

4
00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:23.280
No matter what type of application it is, it typically sits on top of the operating system

5
00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:29.280
and relies on the services of the operating system to execute it, and it does something.

6
00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:33.280
On the mainframe, the magic word is transaction.

7
00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:39.280
The simplest way to think of a transaction is an interaction, typically where something is exchanged.

8
00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:44.280
If you were in a store, the transaction would begin when you say, I want to buy this,

9
00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:50.280
and it would finish when you walk out of the store holding the thing you wanted without getting chased down.

10
00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:54.280
Or if it's an informational transaction, like you're buying an airline ticket,

11
00:00:54.280 --> 00:00:58.280
the transaction starts when you click to book the flight and isn't concluded

12
00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:03.280
until you've got some sort of confirmation with a booking ID or something you can reference.

13
00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:08.280
Additionally, if someone were to come along and try to book the same seat on the same flight,

14
00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:11.280
they wouldn't be able to because you already got it.

15
00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:16.280
What I'm getting at is that at the business level, it's important that there be a clear beginning

16
00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:21.280
and end point of a transaction, and the same goes for all the smaller technical transactions

17
00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:24.280
that make up the overall business transaction.

18
00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:30.280
The characteristics of a transaction can best be described by how they adhere to the ACID properties.

19
00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:36.280
ACID stands for atomicity, consistency, isolation, and durability, one at a time.

20
00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:42.280
Atomicity, that's a way of saying that either a transaction happened or it didn't.

21
00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:46.280
Since a transaction can have multiple smaller steps or transactions within it,

22
00:01:46.280 --> 00:01:50.280
it's important that all of those complete before we consider the transaction done,

23
00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:54.280
so there's no way to go back later and say, well, actually, we took your money,

24
00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:57.280
but we never actually gave you what you wanted.

25
00:01:57.280 --> 00:02:02.280
Consistency means that the database or the data related to a transaction

26
00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:06.280
only moves between one valid state and another valid state,

27
00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:11.280
and at any time, all the data can be frozen, gone over with a fine-tooth comb,

28
00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:15.280
and it would all check out and make sense according to its rules.

29
00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:20.280
An inconsistent database is one where bad programming could potentially leave data

30
00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:26.280
in unacceptable places or update values in undocumented or unsupported ways.

31
00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:28.280
Number three is isolation.

32
00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:34.280
It's generally understood that a system will have lots and lots of transactions happening at the same time,

33
00:02:34.280 --> 00:02:40.280
and there's a fair amount of scheduling and threading and magic that makes this all possible.

34
00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:44.280
Isolation is a measure of how smoothly a single transaction can happen

35
00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:48.280
without being affected by all the other transactions around it,

36
00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:51.280
and this isn't necessarily a performance statement,

37
00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:56.280
but how often am I going to run into tables that are locked because other people are using them

38
00:02:56.280 --> 00:02:59.280
or seeing data that is meant to be temporary?

39
00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:05.280
Put another way, how well can the system scale while keeping complexity down?

40
00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:07.280
And then D, durability.

41
00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:10.280
How permanent are successful transactions?

42
00:03:10.280 --> 00:03:12.280
Sometimes software crashes.

43
00:03:12.280 --> 00:03:15.280
Maybe someone thought they were logged into the test system

44
00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:19.280
when they were actually logged into the production system and they brought down database.

45
00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:22.280
Or maybe a cable got pulled that wasn't supposed to get pulled

46
00:03:22.280 --> 00:03:24.280
or someone backed a forklift into a DASD.

47
00:03:24.280 --> 00:03:25.280
Stuff happens.

48
00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:28.280
Anyway, the transactions that went through,

49
00:03:28.280 --> 00:03:31.280
will they still be there when the system comes back up?

50
00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:33.280
Or will you have to say,

51
00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:39.280
hey, anybody who made updates in the last 30 minutes should check and make sure they're still there?

52
00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:40.280
You don't want to do that.

53
00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:43.280
That is the measure of durability.

54
00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:45.280
You've got all four things,

55
00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:49.280
atomicity, consistency, isolation, and durability.

56
00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:54.280
You've got yourself an excellent platform for important business transactions.

57
00:03:54.280 --> 00:03:56.280
This is an industry-wide concept.

58
00:03:56.280 --> 00:03:58.280
It's not just the mainframe thing.

59
00:03:58.280 --> 00:04:02.280
You can look at any solution and consider its acidity.

60
00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:08.280
So if I were looking for an acid property that ensures that all of the data in a database is valid,

61
00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:13.280
explainable, and done according to the documented rules for that database,

62
00:04:13.280 --> 00:04:17.280
which acid property would you be looking for?

63
00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:19.280
That's the consistency property.

64
00:04:19.280 --> 00:04:22.280
Without it, you get transactions halfway done,

65
00:04:22.280 --> 00:04:27.280
money being withdrawn from one account without going anywhere else and all sorts of other bad stuff.

66
00:04:27.280 --> 00:04:29.280
So, consistency.

67
00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:30.280
Pretty important.

68
00:04:30.280 --> 00:04:33.280
Because once your database is corrupted,

69
00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:37.280
any further work could be deemed invalid.

70
00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:43.280
On the mainframe, a transaction processing manager or transaction manager exists to tie together

71
00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:47.280
many transactions that are required to fulfill a larger transaction.

72
00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:51.280
So in this example, the travel agency sits here in the middle

73
00:04:51.280 --> 00:04:54.280
and exists to make sure that you get booked on the flight you want

74
00:04:54.280 --> 00:04:56.280
with the hotel reservation you wanted,

75
00:04:56.280 --> 00:04:58.280
and you get car rentals where you need them.

76
00:04:58.280 --> 00:05:03.280
For the consumer, the transaction isn't complete until everything is complete

77
00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:07.280
and you feel confident that your vacation or business trip is booked.

78
00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:10.280
So what magic makes this possible?

79
00:05:10.280 --> 00:05:15.280
In the next lesson, we'll dig into some of the middleware that powers transactions on the mainframe.
